(@user_doukan_gnzeli, @clinic_id, NULL, 'google_maps',
    'places/ChIJreSV2_frTRMR8ouwS60TQbI/reviews/Ci9DQUlRQUNvZENodHljRjlvT2pkTFJXSm5SRXRpYTBKWWVHVjNZVlZtYnpWeVYwRRAB',
    2, 'en', 'I should have walked out immediately when I realized that the dentist didn’t understand what I was trying to explain about my toothache. Unfortunately, I chose to trust him, and that was my mistake.

I was there about two weeks ago. There was nothing visibly wrong with my tooth from the outside, but apparently there was decay inside, and it was causing pain whenever pressure was applied. I explained this to him exactly as it was, but he decided to examine the tooth first without anesthesia. As he started drilling, he realized that there was decay and then told me that we needed to use anesthesia. I simply left everything in his hands because he was the dentist, after all.

Unfortunately, even after he administered anesthesia, I could still feel the pain whenever he touched the root of my tooth. In the middle of the procedure, he decided to take an X-ray — something that should have been done at the very beginning. After seeing the X-ray, he said there might be an infection, although he still wasn’t sure.

He tried administering anesthesia from different areas probably four or five times, and when he realized that it wasn’t working, he closed the tooth with a temporary filling and told me that he couldn’t figure out what was going on. Then he simply sent me home. My jaw hurt for about two days because of all the injections.

But the worst part is that my tooth broke the following evening, and it doesn’t look like it can be saved. I’m still waiting for an appointment with another dentist.

In short, I’m not saying I wouldn’t recommend him — I’m just grateful I didn’t let him go any further with the treatment.',
    NULL, NULL, 'I should have walked out immediately when I realized that the dentist didn’t understand what I was trying to explain about my toothache. Unfortunately, I chose to trust him, and that was my mistake.

I was there about two weeks ago. There was nothing visibly wrong with my tooth from the outside, but apparently there was decay inside, and it was causing pain whenever pressure was applied. I explained this to him exactly as it was, but he decided to examine the tooth first without anesthesia. As he started drilling, he realized that there was decay and then told me that we needed to use anesthesia. I simply left everything in his hands because he was the dentist, after all.

Unfortunately, even after he administered anesthesia, I could still feel the pain whenever he touched the root of my tooth. In the middle of the procedure, he decided to take an X-ray — something that should have been done at the very beginning. After seeing the X-ray, he said there might be an infection, although he still wasn’t sure.

He tried administering anesthesia from different areas probably four or five times, and when he realized that it wasn’t working, he closed the tooth with a temporary filling and told me that he couldn’t figure out what was going on. Then he simply sent me home. My jaw hurt for about two days because of all the injections.

But the worst part is that my tooth broke the following evening, and it doesn’t look like it can be saved. I’m still waiting for an appointment with another dentist.

In short, I’m not saying I wouldn’t recommend him — I’m just grateful I didn’t let him go any further with the treatment.', NULL, NULL, NULL,
    0, '2026-08-30 00:00:00'),

